Subject: [PATCH v4 0/4] lab-process-mail: Add lab-process-maild
To: Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com>
Cc: lab-devel@p7x.dev
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 03:51:04 +0000

lab-process-mail answers the inbox only when someone runs it. This
series makes it a one-shot program and adds lab-process-maild, which
manages it as a service under daemon(8):

    lab-process-maild start
    lab-process-maild status
    lab-process-maild stop

lab-process-maild loops, sleeps, and waits on nothing. start hands
lab-process-mail to daemon(8), which detaches it, keeps the log and
the pidfile, and with -R runs it again lab_process_maild_interval
seconds after each exit. stop signals daemon(8), which passes SIGTERM
on to lab-process-mail. The script is now start, stop, status, and the
conf.

The interface is unchanged otherwise. It ships in the lab-process-mail
package with lab-process-maild(8), and reads the same rc.conf(5)-style
file, ${LOCALBASE}/etc/lab-process-maild.conf, with the same four
settings and defaults. daemon(8) starts from an empty environment,
keeping only HOME, USER, LOGNAME, and PATH as the operator has it, plus
lab_process_maild_env. Nothing is added to PATH; the conf is the one
place to change it:

    lab_process_maild_env="LAB_BASE_BRANCH=b/dev@upstream \
        NOTIFY_URL=https://ntfy.sh/my-topic \
        PATH=/bin:/usr/bin:/usr/local/bin"

Two behaviors the daemon used to provide now live in lab-process-mail,
since daemon(8) only restarts its child:

- A stuck message is held rather than retried. daemon(8) reruns the
  child after any exit, failed or not (checked on 15.1), so it cannot
  stop on failure as v3 did. Instead lab-process-mail records the
  stuck message in ~/var/db/lab-process-mail/stuck, and while it is
  still the oldest in the inbox, later runs fail before the agent
  starts. A held run costs a fetch, not an agent pass, and sends no
  new notice. Other failures, such as a network outage, are retried
  after one interval, which covers the transient case in
  "[BUG] lab-process-mail gets stuck" [1].
- SIGTERM lets the pass in progress finish. lab-process-mail traps it
  and exits once its pass is done, or before starting one. I checked
  that daemon(8) waits for a child that does this, keeps the pidfile
  until then, and does not restart it.

Patch 1 makes lab-process-mail answer one message and exit. Patches 2
and 3 add the hold and the SIGTERM handling, and patch 4 adds the
daemon.

kyua test passes at every patch: 15/15, 18/18, 20/20, and 29/29. The
daemon tests run the real daemon(8) for start, a restart after a
failed run, status, and stop, and a stub for the rest. mandoc -Tlint
is clean apart from the stale system mandoc.db.

Changes in v4:
- Run lab-process-mail directly under daemon(8) -R and drop the run
  command, with its backgrounded child, wait, and sleep loop. Pat
  pointed out that this conflated service management with
  daemonization.
- Stop baking a PATH into start. PATH passes through from the caller,
  and lab_process_maild_env can replace it. daemon(8) itself is found
  with env -P, so a PATH in the conf cannot hide it.
- Replace "a failed run stops the daemon", which daemon(8) cannot do,
  with the stuck-message hold in lab-process-mail (new patch 2).
- Let lab-process-mail finish its pass on SIGTERM (new patch 3), which
  the run loop's trap used to provide.
- Move "Answer one message per run" first, since the daemon builds on
  it, and drop the inbox check it added to the run loop; a batch of
  mail is now answered one message per interval.
- Link to v3: id:cover.1792207350.git.claude@p7x.dev

